name: reachability
boss: Portbreaker
flavor: I count the doors before you knock.
brief: Four questions, in order, when something will not connect: is the link
brief: up, does it have an address, is there a route, does dns work. Answer
brief: them in that order and you will almost never guess.
brief:
brief: ping proves a host answers. traceroute shows where the packets stop.
brief: netstat shows what your own machine is doing about it.
---
q: {send three pings to @ip@ and stop|ping @ip@ three times, then stop|send exactly three pings to @ip@}
a: ping -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -c
hint: -c sets how many pings to send before stopping
---
q: {ping @ip@ quietly, printing only the summary|ping @ip@ with per-packet output suppressed, summary only|ping @ip@ and print only the final summary}
a: ping -c 3 -q @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -q
hint: -q suppresses per-packet lines, printing only the final summary
---
q: {ping with one second between packets|ping @ip@, spacing packets one second apart|space out the pings to @ip@ by one second each}
a: ping -i 1 -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -i
hint: -i sets the interval between packets, in seconds
---
q: {ping out of a specific interface|ping @ip@ through a chosen interface|send the pings to @ip@ out of a specific interface}
a: ping -I @iface@ -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -I
hint: -I picks which interface the pings go out of
---
q: {ping with a payload of 1000 bytes to test for MTU trouble|ping @ip@ with a 1000 byte payload, to probe for MTU trouble|send 1000 byte pings to @ip@ to test for MTU issues}
a: ping -s 1000 -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -s
hint: -s sets the payload size in bytes, useful for MTU testing
---
q: {give up on the whole ping run after 5 seconds|cap the whole ping run to @ip@ at 5 seconds|stop pinging @ip@ after 5 seconds, whatever happens}
a: ping -w 5 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -w
hint: -w caps the whole run's wall-clock time, in seconds
---
q: {wait at most 2 seconds for each individual reply|cap the wait for each reply from @ip@ at 2 seconds|ping @ip@, waiting no more than 2 seconds per reply}
a: ping -W 2 -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -W
hint: -W caps how long to wait for each individual reply
---
q: {ping with a fixed time to live of 5 hops|ping @ip@ with the ttl fixed at 5 hops|send pings to @ip@ that expire after 5 hops}
a: ping -t 5 -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -t
hint: -t sets the ip ttl, capping how many hops a packet can cross
---
q: {ping using a chosen pattern in the payload bytes|ping @ip@ with a specific byte pattern filling the payload|fill the ping payload to @ip@ with a chosen pattern}
a: ping -p ff -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -p
hint: -p fills the payload with a repeating hex pattern you choose
---
q: {ping in adaptive mode, sending as fast as replies come back|ping @ip@ adaptively, firing the next packet as soon as a reply lands|speed up pings to @ip@, sending as fast as replies allow}
a: ping -A -c 3 @ip@
ref: ping
teach: ping -A
hint: -A sends the next ping as soon as the last reply arrives, instead of waiting
---
q: %show% the path packets take to @ip@
a: traceroute @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute
hint: traceroute with no flags shows the path packets take, hop by hop
---
q: {trace the path without resolving names, so it runs fast|trace to @ip@ skipping name resolution for speed|run the trace to @ip@ fast, with no hostname lookups}
a: traceroute -n @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -n
hint: -n skips resolving each hop's hostname, so it runs faster
---
q: {trace no further than 10 hops|cap the trace to @ip@ at 10 hops|trace to @ip@ but stop after 10 hops}
a: traceroute -m 10 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -m
hint: -m caps the maximum number of hops traced
---
q: {send one probe per hop instead of three|trace to @ip@ sending just one probe per hop|cut the probes per hop to one, instead of the default three}
a: traceroute -q 1 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -q
hint: -q sets how many probes are sent per hop, default is three
---
q: {start the trace at hop 5, skipping the first four|trace to @ip@ starting from hop 5|skip the first four hops and start tracing at hop 5}
a: traceroute -f 5 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -f
hint: -f sets the starting ttl, skipping the earlier hops
---
q: {trace using icmp echo rather than udp|trace to @ip@ with icmp echo probes instead of udp|switch the trace to @ip@ over to icmp echo}
a: traceroute -I @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -I
hint: -I switches the probe type to icmp echo instead of udp
---
q: {trace out of a specific interface|trace to @ip@ through a chosen interface|send the trace to @ip@ out of a specific interface}
a: traceroute -i @iface@ @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -i
hint: -i picks which interface the probes go out of
---
q: {trace using a chosen source address|trace to @ip@ with a source address you pick|set the source address for a trace to @ip@}
a: traceroute -s @ip2@ @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -s
hint: -s sets the source address the probes claim to be from
---
q: {trace to a specific destination port|trace to @ip@ targeting a specific port|aim the trace to @ip@ at one destination port}
a: traceroute -p @port@ @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -p
hint: -p sets the destination port the probes target
---
q: {wait at most 2 seconds for each probe|cap each probe's wait time at 2 seconds while tracing to @ip@|trace to @ip@, waiting no more than 2 seconds per probe}
a: traceroute -w 2 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -w
hint: -w caps how long to wait for each probe's reply
---
q: {trace verbosely, showing every packet received|trace to @ip@ verbosely, printing every packet received|run a verbose trace to @ip@, showing every packet}
a: traceroute -v @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -v
hint: -v prints every packet received, not just the summary
---
q: {trace bypassing the normal routing tables|trace to @ip@, skipping the normal routing tables|send trace probes to @ip@ direct, bypassing routing}
a: traceroute -r @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -r
hint: -r sends probes directly, bypassing the normal routing tables
---
q: {pause half a second between probes|trace to @ip@ with half a second between probes|space the probes to @ip@ half a second apart}
a: traceroute -z 500 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -z
hint: -z pauses between probes, in milliseconds
---
q: {set the time to live of the first probe|trace to @ip@, setting the first probe's ttl|choose the ttl the very first probe to @ip@ carries}
a: traceroute -t 1 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -t
hint: -t sets the ttl of the very first probe
---
q: {set the probe packet length|trace to @ip@ with a chosen probe packet length|set how long each probe packet to @ip@ is}
a: traceroute -l 60 @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -l
hint: -l sets the length of the probe packets themselves
---
q: {do not fragment the trace packets|trace to @ip@ with fragmentation disabled|forbid the trace packets to @ip@ from being fragmented}
a: traceroute -F @ip@
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -F
hint: -F sets the don't-fragment bit on the trace packets
---
q: {look up the address of a hostname|resolve @host@ to its address|find the address @host@ resolves to}
a: nslookup @host@
ref: nslookup
teach: nslookup
hint: nslookup with just a name asks your configured resolver
---
q: {look up a name using a specific dns server|resolve @host@ using a dns server you name|query a specific dns server directly for @host@}
a: nslookup @host@ @ip@
ref: nslookup
teach: nslookup
hint: naming a server after the host queries it directly, bypassing your resolver
---
q: {what sockets is this machine using|report the sockets this machine is currently using|list this machine's current sockets}
a: netstat
ref: netstat
teach: netstat
hint: netstat alone lists the sockets currently in use
---
q: %show% only listening sockets
a: netstat -l
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -l
hint: -l filters down to sockets in the listening state
---
q: %show% every socket, listening or not
a: netstat -a
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -a
hint: -a shows every socket, listening and connected alike
---
q: %show% sockets numerically, without resolving names or ports
a: netstat -n
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -n
hint: -n skips resolving hostnames and service names to numbers
---
q: %show% only tcp sockets
a: netstat -t
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -t
hint: -t filters to tcp sockets only
---
q: %show% only udp sockets
a: netstat -u
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -u
hint: -u filters to udp sockets only
---
q: %show% which process owns each socket
a: netstat -p
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -p
hint: -p adds the pid and process name that owns each socket
---
q: %show% every listening tcp socket numerically, with its process
a: netstat -tlnp
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -t
hint: single-letter flags combine -- tlnp is t, l, n and p together
---
q: %show% the routing table using netstat
a: netstat -r
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -r
hint: -r switches netstat to print the routing table instead of sockets
---
q: %show% unix domain sockets
a: netstat -x
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -x
hint: -x filters to unix domain sockets
---
q: %show% raw sockets
a: netstat -w
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -w
hint: -w filters to raw sockets
---
q: %show% netstat output with extended information
a: netstat -e
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -e
hint: -e adds extended fields like user and inode
---
q: %show% netstat with wide columns so long addresses are not truncated
a: netstat -W
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -W
hint: -W widens columns so long addresses print in full
---
q: {ping an ipv6 host three times|send three pings to an ipv6 host|ping an ipv6 host, stopping after three}
a: ping6 -c 3 ::1
ref: ping6
teach: ping6
hint: the whole world is moving to ipv6; the tools have a 6 on the end
---
q: {trace the ipv6 path to a host|trace the path to an ipv6 host|show the ipv6 path a trace takes to a host}
a: traceroute6 ::1
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6
hint: traceroute6 is traceroute's ipv6-only counterpart
---
q: {trace the ipv6 path without resolving names|trace the ipv6 path fast, skipping name resolution|trace to an ipv6 host without resolving hostnames}
a: traceroute6 -n ::1
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6 -n
hint: -n skips name resolution here too
---
q: {trace an ipv6 path no further than 10 hops|cap an ipv6 trace at 10 hops|trace the ipv6 path but stop after 10 hops}
a: traceroute6 -m 10 ::1
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6 -m
hint: -m caps hops the same way it does for traceroute
